			<p class="article-meta">Published July 2026 • 6 min read • Verified by GridPermit Energy Audits</p>
		</div>

		<div class="article-body">
			<p class="lead-paragraph">
				When California’s Public Utilities Commission (CPUC) passed NEM 3.0, the economics of residential solar changed overnight. Daytime export credits dropped by over 75%, effectively killing the "solar-only" payback model.
			</p>

			<h2>The NEM 3.0 Math Problem: Why Solar Only Fails</h2>
			<p>
				Under the old NEM 2.0 rules, excess electricity produced by your roof during midday was exported to the grid in exchange for full retail credit. Under NEM 3.0, utilities like PG&E, SCE, and SDG&E credit you an average of only <strong>$0.05 to $0.08 per kWh</strong> exported during peak solar hours.
			</p>
			<p>
				However, when you buy that same electricity back in the evening between 4 PM and 9 PM, utilities charge you up to <strong>$0.60+ per kWh</strong>. 
			</p>

			<div class="highlight-box">
				<h3>💡 Key Takeaway</h3>
				<p>Without a battery, exporting daytime solar energy to the grid is like selling wholesale for pennies and buying back at luxury retail prices. Solar-only systems now take <strong>10 to 12 years</strong> to reach full payback.</p>
			</div>

			<h2>How Battery Storage Restores Your ROI (4 to 6 Year Payback)</h2>
			<p>
				Adding battery storage (such as a Tesla Powerwall, Enphase IQ, or FranklinWH) changes the game. Instead of exporting cheap solar energy to the utility grid, your excess power charges your home battery during the day.
			</p>
			<p>
				When peak rate hours begin at 4 PM, your battery automatically dispatches power to run your air conditioning, appliances, and EV chargers—completely bypassing expensive grid rates.
			</p>

			<h2>2026 Federal Tax Credit & SGIP Incentives</h2>
			<p>
				To soften the financial transition, homeowners can still claim the <strong>30% Federal Residential Clean Energy Tax Credit</strong> on both solar panels and battery storage installations. Furthermore, local SGIP (Self-Generation Incentive Program) rebates provide additional cash-back savings for qualifying medical or equity budget areas.
			</p>

